The US Constitution guarantees several human rights through various amendments. Some of these rights include: 

- Freedom of speech, religion, press, assembly, and petition (First Amendment);
- Right to bear arms (Second Amendment); 
- Protection against quartering of soldiers (Third Amendment); 
- Protection against search and seizure without a warrant (Fourth Amendment); 
- Protection against self-incrimination, double jeopardy, and certain rights in criminal prosecutions (Fifth Amendment);
- Right to a speedy and public trial, right to an impartial jury, and the right to confront witnesses (Sixth Amendment); 
- Right to a trial by jury in civil cases (Seventh Amendment); 
- Protection against excessive bail, fines, and cruel and unusual punishment (Eighth Amendment); 
- Protection of rights not specified in the Constitution (Ninth Amendment); 
- Rights reserved to the states or the people (Tenth Amendment).
